  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1243 PM MDT Sun May 17 2026\n\nISOLATED SEVERE STORMS TODAY:\n\nAnother round of showers and thunderstorms are expected through the \nevening today while increased instability exists across the region. \nThe 18Z sounding from GJT shows an increase in low level moisture, \nand an increase in PWATs, which should support more rain\nactually reaching the ground when compared to yesterday \nafternoon. Strong outflow winds, brief periods of heavy rain, \nand small hail are the biggest concerns with today's storms.\n\nWET THROUGH MONDAY:\n\nA low pressure system moves across the CWA tonight, bringing a \ntransition from afternoon convection to more stratiform like \nprecipitation. This includes widespread rain and high elevation \nsnow. The center of the low, and greatest forcing, moves across  \nUtah late tonight, so precipitation rates are forecast to be the\ngreatest between midnight and noon tomorrow. Snow totals favor \nthe eastern Uinta mountains, where a Winter Weather Advisory is \nin place above 8500 feet from 6 PM this evening through 6 PM \ntomorrow. A Winter Weather Advisory is also in place for the \nElkhead and Park Mountains above 9500 feet from midnight tonight\nto 9 PM tomorrow. By tomorrow afternoon the system will be east\nof our area, however, lingering moisture could allow for some \nlight snowfall in the higher terrain though the day. \n\nFREEZE POTENTIAL: \n\nCold air advection associated with the low pressure system moving \nthrough the area tonight will significantly drop temperatures. \nTomorrow's high temperatures will be around 15 to 25 degrees \nbelow normal. Tomorrow night's low temperatures will also be \nwell below normal for this time of the year. Freeze Watches are \nin effect for late tomorrow night through Tuesday morning for \nthe Central and Lower Yampa River Basin. Temperatures will\ngradually warm back to above normal by the weekend.",
